WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:04.062
Sam was excited as he approached Delancy's Chocolate Factory.

2
00:00:04.063 --> 00:00:09.405
It was his favorite confectionery, and he was looking forward to buying chocolates for Valentine's Day.

3
00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:15.945
Inside, the aroma of chocolate and vanilla filled the air, and chocolate treats packed the shelves.

4
00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:29.494
Sam saw tantalizing dark chocolate bonbons, mint truffles, milk chocolate bars, chocolate-covered cherries, and hundreds of other types of candy, all sitting in pretty trays or wrapped in colorful, shiny paper.

5
00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:38.273
At the counter, Mrs. Delancy was busy filling heart-shaped boxes, while behind her, Mr. Delancy poured chocolate into tiny candy molds.

6
00:00:38.274 --> 00:00:47.417
As Sam looked around at all the chocolate, he wondered, "Where does it come from, how is it made, and why does it taste so good?"

7
00:00:47.418 --> 00:00:49.425
The Beginnings of Chocolate

8
00:00:49.426 --> 00:00:54.299
Believe it or not, chocolate was first consumed as a drink, not as candy.

9
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.783
The story of chocolate begins three thousand years ago in Central America.

10
00:00:58.784 --> 00:01:10.567
The Mayans, who lived in present-day Mexico, had a sophisticated civilization, with huge temples, precise calendars, and a hieroglyphic writing system like the writing of the Ancient Egyptians.

11
00:01:10.568 --> 00:01:13.305
The Mayans also had a taste for chocolate.

12
00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:21.131
They were the first people to cultivate the cacao tree and collect its fruit, the cocoa bean, from which chocolate is made.

13
00:01:21.132 --> 00:01:25.726
They crushed the cocoa beans and mixed them with water to make a refreshing drink.

14
00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:31.405
This cocoa drink was considered very special and reserved for wealthy nobles and warriors.

15
00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:38.929
The Aztecs, whose civilization came to power after the Mayan civilization crumbled, also enjoyed a chocolate drink.

16
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:45.991
They called it chocolatl , drank it hot rather than cold, and often added flavors, such as chili

17
00:01:45.992 --> 00:01:53.824
peppers. The cocoa bean was so valuable to the Aztecs that they used it as a form of currency—money really did grow on trees!

18
00:01:53.825 --> 00:01:56.102
How Chocolate Traveled to Europe

19
00:01:56.103 --> 00:02:00.086
The Aztecs were the first to introduce the special drink to Europeans.

20
00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:07.197
When Christopher Columbus first reached the Americas in 1492, he tried the cocoa drink but was not impressed.

21
00:02:07.198 --> 00:02:10.030
He declared it uninteresting and rather bitter.

22
00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:16.183
The plants used to make sugar did not grow in America, so the chocolate that the Aztecs drank was unsweetened.

23
00:02:16.184 --> 00:02:25.509
A subsequent explorer, Hernando Cortes from Spain, found chocolatl delicious and decided to bring it back to Spain for the king to try.

24
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.628
When it was presented to him, the chocolate drink did not impress the king.

25
00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:35.841
To make it more appetizing, a candy maker decided to add sugar, and it was an instant hit.

26
00:02:35.842 --> 00:02:38.667
Chocolate became the rage across Spain.

27
00:02:38.668 --> 00:02:43.262
The first chocolate makers were Spanish monks, who lived hidden in monasteries.

28
00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:49.933
The king of Spain wanted to keep chocolate a secret from the rest of Europe, and the secret lasted for over one hundred years.

29
00:02:49.934 --> 00:03:01.661
When Queen Maria Theresa of Spain married King Louis XIV of France in 1660, she brought chocolate to France, and it wasn't long before its popularity spread across Europe.

30
00:03:01.662 --> 00:03:11.651
Soon, Europe was full of upscale "chocolate houses," where people could sit down and enjoy the novel drink—just as people enjoy coffee in cafés today.

31
00:03:11.652 --> 00:03:14.020
How Chocolate Reached the Masses

32
00:03:14.021 --> 00:03:24.444
Because both cocoa beans and sugar were very expensive, the new cocoa drink was available only to the elite of Europe, just as it had been in the Mayan and Aztec civilizations.

33
00:03:24.445 --> 00:03:30.658
But in the nineteenth century, a few inventions made it available to the many who couldn't afford it before.

34
00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:39.944
A Dutch inventor by the name of Conrad J. Van Houten built a special press that separated the cocoa seeds into cocoa butter and cocoa solids.

35
00:03:39.945 --> 00:03:45.508
The resulting solids could be pulverized into a fine powder, which we now call cocoa.

36
00:03:45.509 --> 00:03:57.145
Cocoa powder not only made chocolate drinks easier to make, it also made it possible to mix the chocolate with sugar and then recombine it with the cocoa butter to form a solid.

37
00:03:57.146 --> 00:04:04.535
Chocolate makers eagerly adopted the new process, and the first chocolate bar appeared in England in 1847.

38
00:04:04.536 --> 00:04:09.087
The Hershey Company was one of the first companies to mass-produce chocolate bars.

39
00:04:09.088 --> 00:04:16.241
It still produces some of today's most popular candy bars, including Kit Kats and Reese's Peanut Butter Cups.

40
00:04:16.242 --> 00:04:19.300
Another innovation involved the addition of milk.

41
00:04:19.301 --> 00:04:26.959
A chocolate maker by the name of Daniel Peter from Switzerland wanted to make a lighter chocolate and added evaporated milk to the mix.

42
00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.411
He first experimented with adding cheese, but apparently, that idea wasn't successful.

43
00:04:32.412 --> 00:04:37.398
Another invention that allowed the popularity of chocolate to spread was the refrigerator.

44
00:04:37.399 --> 00:04:45.778
When refrigeration became widely available in North America in the early 1900s, chocolate became easy to obtain all year-round.

45
00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:47.952
Chocolate in the Modern World

46
00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:57.442
Even though these inventions made chocolate more accessible and easier to make, the main steps of the process have not changed since the Mayans used it three millennia ago.

47
00:04:57.443 --> 00:05:00.760
Cocoa beans grow in pods on the cacao tree.

48
00:05:00.761 --> 00:05:05.100
The pods are collected, allowed to ferment, dried, and then roasted.

49
00:05:05.101 --> 00:05:11.036
Next, the pods are cracked to separate the shells from the beans inside, which are called nibs.

50
00:05:11.037 --> 00:05:14.917
The nibs are ground into a thick paste called chocolate liquor.

51
00:05:14.918 --> 00:05:19.829
It is this chocolate liquor that is pressed to separate the cocoa powder from the cocoa butter. Cocoa

52
00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:33.517
powder and sugar are then recombined with the cocoa butter in controlled amounts to make a smooth, chocolatey paste that can be shaped into chocolate bars, poured into molds, or used to coat other sweets, such as caramels. Chocolate

53
00:05:33.518 --> 00:05:39.202
is one of the most popular types of candy, and it is enjoyed by people of all ages. It

54
00:05:39.203 --> 00:05:43.615
is often presented as a token of appreciation or a special gift. Chocolate

55
00:05:43.616 --> 00:05:46.748
is the traditional gift on Valentine's Day. In

56
00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:51.176
fact, some say that chocolate helps people fall in love. Chocolate

57
00:05:51.177 --> 00:05:54.437
is the favorite flavor for birthday cakes. In

58
00:05:54.438 --> 00:05:58.936
many different countries, kids receive chocolates to celebrate holidays. In

59
00:05:58.937 --> 00:06:06.333
the United States, every Halloween, children race from door to door to collect chocolate and other sweets from their neighbors. On

60
00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:12.253
Saint Nicholas Day, Dutch children get chocolates that are shaped like the first letter of their name. At

61
00:06:12.254 --> 00:06:21.822
Christmas time, German kids receive a calendar with a chocolate hidden under each day's date, which they open one at a time as Christmas Day approaches. On

62
00:06:21.823 --> 00:06:26.820
Easter, kids around the world participate in the hunt for chocolate Easter eggs. Chocolate

63
00:06:26.821 --> 00:06:30.193
is not always used as a sweet treat, however. In

64
00:06:30.194 --> 00:06:38.014
Mexico, some very popular dishes use mole , which is a chocolate sauce; it is often used on chicken, for example.

65
00:06:38.015 --> 00:06:45.069
Of course, the most popular way to enjoy chocolate is as a candy—the best candy of them all, Sam thought.

66
00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:52.919
He decided to get some heart-shaped chocolate bonbons, chocolate-covered almonds, and raspberry truffles for his special Valentine.

67
00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.578
But he couldn't resist and got some chocolate-covered toffee for himself too.

